{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gt9RWzYqv8qzoZrk52btGa","type":"tool_result","content":"395\t  verdict validator does not bind a declared version to its payload hash.\n396\t  The hook fixtures for Codex as a hook host still date from 0.139.0. No\n397\t  release is assigned to its cure yet.\n398\t- **Files merged without the signing ceremony.** The 233 canonical paths\n399\t  that pull requests #6 and #7 brought, the kernel paths among them, are\n400\t  covered by no signed sentinel, and `main` has no protection that would\n401\t  prevent a repeat. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n402\t- **The audit spool.** The exit-drain change is partial: per-process files\n403\t  remain, part of the planned cure is pending, and its stress run is not\n404\t  complete. Whether every skipped event is collected later is not proven.\n405\t  No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n406\t- **The new Bash guard.** Its replay acceptance against the historical\n407\t  command corpus, the private vocabulary row and the OS-sandbox\n408\t  measurements did not run on this implementation. No release is assigned\n409\t  to its cure yet.\n410\t- **Backups and restore.** The key material (`audit-key`, `.salt`) is\n411\t  still not archived. A restore with no `.sha256` file proceeds with a\n412\t  warning. Without `--dest`, an applied restore overwrites the live audit\n413\t  logs while the chain-state files next to them stay as they were.\n414\t  Restore refuses `audit-key` and `.salt` in any letter case, but an\n415\t  HFS+ destination treats more names as equal than the default macOS\n416\t  disk (APFS) does, and there a crafted archive can still reach those\n417\t  files. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n418\t- **The redactor runs before parsing on the way in.** The ingress path\n419\t  redacts the raw answer text before parsing it, so a redaction can change\n420\t  whether an answer parses. This predates the redactor fix above. Its\n421\t  structural cure, redacting the decoded values after parsing, is scheduled\n422\t  for the next release.\n423\t\n424\t### Known-open (carried)\n425\t\n426\t- **The v1.4.0 annex.**\n427\t  - The signed v1.4.0 verdict carries an annex of P1 findings and declared\n428\t    conditions. v1.4.3-rc.1 retired signed condition 67, and that cure is\n429\t    part of this release.\n430\t  - Signed condition 58 is the redactor offsets defect fixed above.\n431\t  - This release does not claim the cure of any other annex condition. The\n432\t    partial spool change above is not claimed as one.\n433\t  - **The rest stays open, and no release is assigned to their cure.**\n434\t  - The list is in the sources the `[1.4.2]` entry names.\n435\t- **The v1.4.2 rc.1 annex.** This release does not claim the cure of its\n436\t  three P1 findings or of its P2 findings, declared in the `[1.4.3]` entry.\n437\t- **The rest of the `[1.4.3]` Known-open stays open as written there**,\n438\t  except where this release changes it:\n439\t  - the restore and strict-mode defects (above; the restore defects\n440\t    partly);\n441\t  - the harness sub-check gaps and the sub-check delegation (above; the\n442\t    sub-check partly);\n443\t  - the plan-lifecycle names and the cost-envelope directory (above);\n444\t  - the Bash guard's indirect-execution case (above, partly);\n445\t  - the root `CLAUDE.md` exclusion (above, partly);\n446\t  - the audit spool (above, partly);\n447\t  - the Codex pin and Codex as a hook host (Security and governance,\n448\t    above, partly): a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version now passes\n449\t    on its declared version without a re-pin, and the pin range, which\n450\t    admitted up to 0.160.x, has no upper bound.\n451\t- **Audit files can be deleted by Claude Code.** Claude Code's cleanup\n452\t  sweep unlinks every top-level `*.jsonl` in each project directory that is\n453\t  older than the `cleanupPeriodDays` of the session running the sweep\n454\t  (default 30), the audit logs and their rotated archives included. There\n455\t  is no plan in this release with a structural cure. The maintainer settings\n456\t  template still ships 90; the framework repository's own settings now use\n457\t  3650, and installs do not receive that file. The protection is still a\n458\t  scheduled backup with `ceo-backup.sh`.\n459\t\n460\t### What an adopter should know before installing or upgrading\n461\t\n462\t- Coming from v1.4.2, read `[1.4.3]` too. Its notes apply.\n463\t- The requirements are unchanged: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later, Python 3.9\n464\t  or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim.\n465\t- A command that reaches `rm -rf`, `git reset --hard` or a forced\n466\t  `git push` indirectly may now be blocked, and so may a shell body the\n467\t  guard cannot parse. To turn the scan off, set\n468\t  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0`, or only the heuristics with\n469\t  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0`, in the environment Claude Code starts\n470\t  with. Each disable is recorded once per session.\n471\t- If you patched a hook by hand, re-apply the patch to its module. The\n472\t  modules behind the seven kernel entry points, the five flat modules\n473\t  above and `_lib/adapters/live/` are kernel paths now: a sentinel alone\n474\t  no longer unlocks them; they need `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` with\n475\t  `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=I-ACCEPT`.\n476\t- If your code reads `Finding.start` and `Finding.end` from\n477\t  `secret_patterns.scan()`, they now index the text you passed in, not its\n478\t  NFKC form.\n479\t- Expect a yellow `main_checkout_state` in `/ceo-boot` when your main\n480\t  checkout is on a feature branch.\n481\t- `ceo-restore.sh` previews by default; pass `--apply` to restore. Keep your\n482\t  own copy of `audit-key` and `.salt`, because the backup leaves them out.\n483\t- The VETO floor now admits retired model ids. If your veto roles must not\n484\t  run on them, check the `model:` of your agent files yourself."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"15f68acd-8563-46de-ac1c-c65891f7fb4e","uuid":"3a5e3492-a724-43ae-90a8-5079efd323cc","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:10:48.990Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"  verdict validator does not bind a declared version to its payload hash.\n  The hook fixtures for Codex as a hook host still date from 0.139.0. No\n  release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **Files merged without the signing ceremony.** The 233 canonical paths\n  that pull requests #6 and #7 brought, the kernel paths among them, are\n  covered by no signed sentinel, and `main` has no protection that would\n  prevent a repeat. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The audit spool.** The exit-drain change is partial: per-process files\n  remain, part of the planned cure is pending, and its stress run is not\n  complete. Whether every skipped event is collected later is not proven.\n  No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The new Bash guard.** Its replay acceptance against the historical\n  command corpus, the private vocabulary row and the OS-sandbox\n  measurements did not run on this implementation. No release is assigned\n  to its cure yet.\n- **Backups and restore.** The key material (`audit-key`, `.salt`) is\n  still not archived. A restore with no `.sha256` file proceeds with a\n  warning. Without `--dest`, an applied restore overwrites the live audit\n  logs while the chain-state files next to them stay as they were.\n  Restore refuses `audit-key` and `.salt` in any letter case, but an\n  HFS+ destination treats more names as equal than the default macOS\n  disk (APFS) does, and there a crafted archive can still reach those\n  files. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The redactor runs before parsing on the way in.** The ingress path\n  redacts the raw answer text before parsing it, so a redaction can change\n  whether an answer parses. This predates the redactor fix above. Its\n  structural cure, redacting the decoded values after parsing, is scheduled\n  for the next release.\n\n### Known-open (carried)\n\n- **The v1.4.0 annex.**\n  - The signed v1.4.0 verdict carries an annex of P1 findings and declared\n    conditions. v1.4.3-rc.1 retired signed condition 67, and that cure is\n    part of this release.\n  - Signed condition 58 is the redactor offsets defect fixed above.\n  - This release does not claim the cure of any other annex condition. The\n    partial spool change above is not claimed as one.\n  - **The rest stays open, and no release is assigned to their cure.**\n  - The list is in the sources the `[1.4.2]` entry names.\n- **The v1.4.2 rc.1 annex.** This release does not claim the cure of its\n  three P1 findings or of its P2 findings, declared in the `[1.4.3]` entry.\n- **The rest of the `[1.4.3]` Known-open stays open as written there**,\n  except where this release changes it:\n  - the restore and strict-mode defects (above; the restore defects\n    partly);\n  - the harness sub-check gaps and the sub-check delegation (above; the\n    sub-check partly);\n  - the plan-lifecycle names and the cost-envelope directory (above);\n  - the Bash guard's indirect-execution case (above, partly);\n  - the root `CLAUDE.md` exclusion (above, partly);\n  - the audit spool (above, partly);\n  - the Codex pin and Codex as a hook host (Security and governance,\n    above, partly): a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version now passes\n    on its declared version without a re-pin, and the pin range, which\n    admitted up to 0.160.x, has no upper bound.\n- **Audit files can be deleted by Claude Code.** Claude Code's cleanup\n  sweep unlinks every top-level `*.jsonl` in each project directory that is\n  older than the `cleanupPeriodDays` of the session running the sweep\n  (default 30), the audit logs and their rotated archives included. There\n  is no plan in this release with a structural cure. The maintainer settings\n  template still ships 90; the framework repository's own settings now use\n  3650, and installs do not receive that file. The protection is still a\n  scheduled backup with `ceo-backup.sh`.\n\n### What an adopter should know before installing or upgrading\n\n- Coming from v1.4.2, read `[1.4.3]` too. Its notes apply.\n- The requirements are unchanged: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later, Python 3.9\n  or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim.\n- A command that reaches `rm -rf`, `git reset --hard` or a forced\n  `git push` indirectly may now be blocked, and so may a shell body the\n  guard cannot parse. To turn the scan off, set\n  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0`, or only the heuristics with\n  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0`, in the environment Claude Code starts\n  with. Each disable is recorded once per session.\n- If you patched a hook by hand, re-apply the patch to its module. The\n  modules behind the seven kernel entry points, the five flat modules\n  above and `_lib/adapters/live/` are kernel paths now: a sentinel alone\n  no longer unlocks them; they need `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` with\n  `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=I-ACCEPT`.\n- If your code reads `Finding.start` and `Finding.end` from\n  `secret_patterns.scan()`, they now index the text you passed in, not its\n  NFKC form.\n- Expect a yellow `main_checkout_state` in `/ceo-boot` when your main\n  checkout is on a feature branch.\n- `ceo-restore.sh` previews by default; pass `--apply` to restore. Keep your\n  own copy of `audit-key` and `.salt`, because the backup leaves them out.\n- The VETO floor now admits retired model ids. If your veto roles must not\n  run on them, check the `model:` of your agent files yourself.","numLines":90,"startLine":395,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Gt9RWzYqv8qzoZrk52btGa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
