{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TP1JgFQADQVTm81Mb2AVB9","type":"tool_result","content":"223\tdef _e4_check_posture_toggle_invocation(command: str) -> Optional[str]:\n224\t    \"\"\"Return a deny reason if ``command`` RUNS a posture-toggle script.\n225\t\n226\t    Pure (no I/O beyond the module-level regexes); safe to call on every\n227\t    command. See the block comment above for the `_e3` division of labour.\n228\t\n229\t    TWO PASSES over the same walk (codex S292 r3 P1 — expansion-built paths):\n230\t\n231\t      1. the LITERAL pass, on the newline-normalised command. Owns every rule\n232\t         that reads the text as written, including the `$(…)`-in-execution-\n233\t         position rule, whose whole point is that the substitution is visible.\n234\t      2. the EXPANSION-SKELETON pass, run ONLY if pass 1 allowed and only when\n235\t         the command carries an expansion at all. Every `${X}` / `$(…)` /\n236\t         `` `…` `` becomes `*`, and the SAME walk asks whether the shell could\n237\t         still land on the toggle there.\n238\t\n239\t    Order is load-bearing in both directions and pinned by\n240\t    ``test_expansion_skeleton_does_not_shadow_the_literal_pass``: pass 1 must\n241\t    run first so `python3 $(echo <toggle>) on` keeps its precise message (its\n242\t    skeleton, `python3 *`, is deliberately allow-shaped), and pass 2 must run\n243\t    at all so `python3 .claude/scripts/night${X}-mode.py on` is refused.\n244\t\n245\t    Cost: pass 2 tokenizes a second time, and only for commands containing a\n246\t    `$` or a backtick. One shlex pass measured 19-39us against the PLAN-089 §4\n247\t    AC8 budget of p95 < 50ms per command — see the note in `_e4_scan`.\n248\t    \"\"\"\n249\t    if not command:\n250\t        return None\n251\t\n252\t    text = _e4_normalise_command(command)\n253\t    reason = _e4_scan(text)\n254\t    if reason is not None:\n255\t        return reason\n256\t\n257\t    if \"$\" in text or \"`\" in text:\n258\t        skeleton = _e4_globify_expansions(text)\n259\t        if skeleton != text:\n260\t            return _e4_scan(skeleton, expansion_pass=True)\n261\t    return None\n262\t\n263\t\n264\tdef _e4_scan(command, expansion_pass=False):  # noqa: ANN001\n265\t    \"\"\"One tokenize-and-walk over ``command``. See the orchestrator above.\n266\t\n267\t    ``expansion_pass`` only changes the deny MESSAGE (and suppresses the\n268\t    unparseable-input branch, which pass 1 has already adjudicated) — the\n269\t    rules themselves are identical, which is the point: a skeleton is\n270\t    judged by the same execution-position discipline as real text, so a\n271\t    READ stays a read.\n272\t    \"\"\"\n273\t    if not command:\n274\t        return None\n275\t\n276\t    # NO raw-substring pre-filter (codex S292 P1-A). The first implementation\n277\t    # short-circuited on `basename in command`, which the SHELL defeats for\n278\t    # free: `night\"-\"mode.py`, `night''-mode.py`, `night\\-mode.py` and\n279\t    # `NIGHT-MODE.PY` all name the real file while the raw string does not\n280\t    # contain the literal basename — every one returned ALLOW. Only the\n281\t    # TOKENIZED, normalised form can answer \"does this run the toggle\", so\n282\t    # the matcher now always tokenizes.\n283\t    #\n284\t    # Cost, measured on this repo (CPython 3.9.6, darwin): one `shlex` pass is\n285\t    # 19-39us on a `decide_command` that already spends 76-188us — and the\n286\t    # governing budget is the PLAN-089 §4 AC8 hook gate of p95 < 50ms per\n287\t    # command (test_check_bash_safety_canonical_matrix.py). The added pass is\n288\t    # ~0.06% of that budget, against a hook whose wall time is dominated by\n289\t    # process spawn. `_e3` above already tokenizes unconditionally with the\n290\t    # identical shlex configuration; sharing one memoized tokenizer between\n291\t    # the two would make the marginal cost ~zero, and was NOT done here on\n292\t    # purpose — it would put `_e3`'s whole matcher inside this patch's blast\n293\t    # radius for 25us that the budget does not need.\n294\t    try:\n295\t        _lx = shlex.shlex(command, posix=True, punctuation_chars=\"();<>|&`\")\n296\t        _lx.whitespace_split = True\n297\t        tokens = list(_lx)\n298\t    except ValueError:\n299\t        # INPUT-parse failure → fail-CLOSED, but only for a command that\n300\t        # plausibly names the toggle (PLAN-152 debate C4). The gate is the\n301\t        # QUOTE/ESCAPE-STRIPPED, case-folded raw string: a broken command\n302\t        # cannot be walked, so this is the widest sound test available, and\n303\t        # it is the one shape where the raw scan is not defeatable by\n304\t        # concatenation. Commands that do NOT name the toggle return None so\n305\t        # they keep `_e3`'s own fail-closed parse message instead of a\n306\t        # confusing night-mode reason.\n307\t        #\n308\t        # NOT on the expansion pass: pass 1 saw the operator's real text and\n309\t        # already decided whether it parses. A skeleton that fails to parse\n310\t        # where the original did is an artefact of `*` substitution, not an\n311\t        # unparseable command, and denying on it would report a shape the\n312\t        # operator never wrote.\n313\t        if expansion_pass:\n314\t            return None\n315\t        flat = command.lower()\n316\t        for _ch in (chr(39), chr(34), \"\\\\\"):\n317\t            flat = flat.replace(_ch, \"\")\n318\t        if any(b in flat for b in _E4_TOGGLE_BASENAMES_FOLDED):\n319\t            return _e4_deny_reason(\"unparseable command naming the toggle\")\n320\t        return None\n321\t\n322\t    n = len(tokens)\n323\t    i = 0\n324\t    at_cmd_word = True      # index 0, and every token after a TERMINATOR\n325\t    chain_runner = None     # the prefix runner (exec/env/sudo/timeout/...) whose\n326\t                            # flags we are currently parsing. Flags and numeric\n327\t                            # operands before the real command word\n328\t                            # (`timeout 5 python3 …`, `nice -n 10 python3 …`)\n329\t                            # are part of the PREFIX, not of a new command.\n330\t    ambiguous = False       # the previous token was a flag the runner's table\n331\t                            # does not carry, so the NEXT token might be its\n332\t                            # value rather than the command word. Fail-closed:\n333\t                            # examine it as a command word AND keep scanning\n334\t                            # one position further (codex S292 P1-B).\n335\t    assigned_toggle = False  # a shell variable in THIS command holds the toggle\n336\t                             # path (`S=.claude/scripts/night-mode.py; …`). shlex\n337\t                             # does not expand `$S`, so the structural rules below\n338\t                             # cannot see through it. Used ONLY in conjunction\n339\t                             # with an execution position — `S=…; cat $S` stays\n340\t                             # ALLOWED, because reading is not invoking.\n341\t    while i < n:\n342\t        tok = tokens[i]\n343\t        if tok in _E3_TERMINATORS:\n344\t            at_cmd_word = True\n345\t            chain_runner = None\n346\t            ambiguous = False\n347\t            i += 1\n348\t            continue\n349\t        if tok in _E3_REDIRECTS:\n350\t            i += 2          # skip the redirect operator + its target\n351\t            continue\n352\t        if not at_cmd_word:\n353\t            # INTERPRETER HOP — the STRUCTURAL rail (codex S292 r5 P1).\n354\t            #\n355\t            # `xcrun python3 <toggle> on` was ALLOWED because `xcrun` is not\n356\t            # in `_E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS`, so the walk stopped at it and never\n357\t            # looked at `python3` again. Measured on this machine: it RUNS,\n358\t            # and the script's own NF-08b self-path guard PASSES for it (the\n359\t            # file executed is the canonical one), so this matcher is the\n360\t            # ONLY rail for the shape. `arch`, `caffeinate` and `script`\n361\t            # behaved identically, and the list had failed the same way twice\n362\t            # before (r2 `env`/`sudo`, r4 braces).\n363\t            #\n364\t            # So stop asking WHO launches and ask WHAT is launched: after an\n365\t            # unrecognised command word, keep testing every remaining token\n366\t            # of the segment for INTERPRETER family. The launcher's name\n367\t            # stops mattering — `<anything> python3 <toggle>` is denied.\n368\t            #\n369\t            # ONLY the family rule fires here, never the direct-exec, `$S` or\n370\t            # substitution rules. That is the line between an invocation and a\n371\t            # read: `cat <toggle>` and `xcrun <toggle>` are the SAME shape to\n372\t            # a string matcher (the shell runs the first word in both; only\n373\t            # the launcher's semantics differ), so applying the direct-exec\n374\t            # rule at hopped positions would deny every read of the file.\n375\t            # Direct exec through a launcher is covered — for the launchers\n376\t            # that could be measured — by `_E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS` instead.\n377\t            #\n378\t            # FALSE POSITIVE, accepted and bounded: an interpreter NAME used\n379\t            # as data in the same command as the toggle now denies, e.g.\n380\t            # `grep -n python3 <toggle>`. It cannot be told apart from\n381\t            # `xcrun python3 <toggle>` without knowing each command's\n382\t            # semantics. It costs one rewrite (`cat <toggle> | grep python3`),\n383\t            # it only ever fires on a command that already names the toggle,\n384\t            # and the deny message says what to do — while the other error\n385\t            # direction is a silent bypass of a rail three signed comments\n386\t            # claim is closed (CLAUDE.md §4: fail-CLOSED on matcher input).\n387\t            hop_family = _e4_runner_family(_e3_cmd_name(tok).lower())\n388\t            if hop_family is not None:\n389\t                hit = _e4_interpreter_deny(\n390\t                    tokens, i, hop_family, assigned_toggle, expansion_pass)\n391\t                if hit is not None:\n392\t                    return hit\n393\t            i += 1\n394\t            continue\n395\t        if _ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE.match(tok):\n396\t            if _e4_is_toggle_path(tok.split(\"=\", 1)[1]):\n397\t                assigned_toggle = True\n398\t            i += 1          # leading NAME=VALUE — the command word is still ahead\n399\t            continue\n400\t        if chain_runner is not None and tok.startswith(\"-\"):\n401\t            # Resolve the runner chain instead of stopping at the first\n402\t            # non-flag token: `env -u FOO python3 <toggle>` used to read FOO\n403\t            # as the command word and never look at python3 again.\n404\t            _vals, _bools = _E4_PREFIX_RUNNER_FLAGS.get(\n405\t                chain_runner, (frozenset(), frozenset()))\n406\t            kind = _e4_classify_prefix_flag(tok, _vals, _bools)\n407\t            if kind == \"value\" and i + 1 < n:\n408\t                i += 2      # the next token is this flag's VALUE, not a command\n409\t                continue\n410\t            ambiguous = kind == \"unknown\"\n411\t            i += 1\n412\t            continue\n413\t        if chain_runner is not None and _E4_DURATION_RE.match(tok):\n414\t            i += 1          # `timeout 5 …` / `nice 10 …` — a prefix operand\n415\t            continue\n416\t        # CASE-FOLDED, for the same reason the PATH is (see\n417\t        # `_e4_runner_family`): PATH lookup is case-insensitive on APFS, so\n418\t        # `PYTHON3`/`SUDO`/`ENV` name the real binaries.\n419\t        name = _e3_cmd_name(tok).lower()\n420\t        if name in _E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS:\n421\t            chain_runner = name\n422\t            ambiguous = False\n423\t            i += 1\n424\t            continue\n425\t        if _e4_is_toggle_path(tok):\n426\t            return _e4_deny_reason(tok, expansion_pass)  # direct exec: `./…/night-mode.py on`\n427\t        if assigned_toggle and \"$\" in tok:\n428\t            return _e4_deny_reason(tok, expansion_pass)  # `S=…; $S on`\n429\t        if tok in _E4_SUBST_OPENERS and _e4_substitution_body_names_toggle(tokens, i):\n430\t            # `$(echo …/night-mode.py) on` — substitution IN COMMAND POSITION.\n431\t            return _e4_deny_reason(tok, expansion_pass)\n432\t        if name in _E4_LINK_RUNNERS:\n433\t            # `ln [-s] <toggle> <alias>` — creating a second entry point.\n434\t            # BOTH operand positions matter: source (the bypass) and target\n435\t            # (replacing the toggle with a link, which `_e3` also denies as a\n436\t            # write). Flags cannot match `_e4_is_toggle_path`, so the segment\n437\t            # positionals are scanned as-is; no flag table is needed.\n438\t            for operand in _e3_segment_positionals(tokens, i + 1):\n439\t                if _e4_is_toggle_path(operand):\n440\t                    return _e4_alias_deny_reason(operand, expansion_pass)\n441\t                if assigned_toggle and \"$\" in operand:\n442\t                    return _e4_alias_deny_reason(operand, expansion_pass)  # `S=…; ln -s $S X`\n443\t            at_cmd_word = False\n444\t            chain_runner = None\n445\t            i += 1\n446\t            continue\n447\t        family = _e4_runner_family(name)\n448\t        if family is not None:\n449\t            hit = _e4_interpreter_deny(\n450\t                tokens, i, family, assigned_toggle, expansion_pass)\n451\t            if hit is not None:\n452\t                return hit\n453\t            at_cmd_word = False\n454\t            chain_runner = None\n455\t            i += 1\n456\t            continue\n457\t        if ambiguous:\n458\t            # This token followed a flag the table does not know, so it may\n459\t            # be that flag's VALUE and the command word may still be ahead.\n460\t            # One unknown flag buys exactly one extra candidate position —\n461\t            # enough to catch `env --frobnicate VAL python3 <toggle>` without\n462\t            # walking off into the settled command's own arguments (which is\n463\t            # what would false-positive on `sudo -u root cat <toggle>`).\n464\t            ambiguous = False\n465\t            i += 1\n466\t            continue\n467\t        at_cmd_word = False\n468\t        chain_runner = None\n469\t        i += 1\n470\t    return None\n471\t\n472\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"15f68acd-8563-46de-ac1c-c65891f7fb4e","uuid":"ccc219e6-b4c6-4c18-94aa-75031e472d1f","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:13:27.301Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_scan.py","content":"def _e4_check_posture_toggle_invocation(command: str) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Return a deny reason if ``command`` RUNS a posture-toggle script.\n\n    Pure (no I/O beyond the module-level regexes); safe to call on every\n    command. See the block comment above for the `_e3` division of labour.\n\n    TWO PASSES over the same walk (codex S292 r3 P1 — expansion-built paths):\n\n      1. the LITERAL pass, on the newline-normalised command. Owns every rule\n         that reads the text as written, including the `$(…)`-in-execution-\n         position rule, whose whole point is that the substitution is visible.\n      2. the EXPANSION-SKELETON pass, run ONLY if pass 1 allowed and only when\n         the command carries an expansion at all. Every `${X}` / `$(…)` /\n         `` `…` `` becomes `*`, and the SAME walk asks whether the shell could\n         still land on the toggle there.\n\n    Order is load-bearing in both directions and pinned by\n    ``test_expansion_skeleton_does_not_shadow_the_literal_pass``: pass 1 must\n    run first so `python3 $(echo <toggle>) on` keeps its precise message (its\n    skeleton, `python3 *`, is deliberately allow-shaped), and pass 2 must run\n    at all so `python3 .claude/scripts/night${X}-mode.py on` is refused.\n\n    Cost: pass 2 tokenizes a second time, and only for commands containing a\n    `$` or a backtick. One shlex pass measured 19-39us against the PLAN-089 §4\n    AC8 budget of p95 < 50ms per command — see the note in `_e4_scan`.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not command:\n        return None\n\n    text = _e4_normalise_command(command)\n    reason = _e4_scan(text)\n    if reason is not None:\n        return reason\n\n    if \"$\" in text or \"`\" in text:\n        skeleton = _e4_globify_expansions(text)\n        if skeleton != text:\n            return _e4_scan(skeleton, expansion_pass=True)\n    return None\n\n\ndef _e4_scan(command, expansion_pass=False):  # noqa: ANN001\n    \"\"\"One tokenize-and-walk over ``command``. See the orchestrator above.\n\n    ``expansion_pass`` only changes the deny MESSAGE (and suppresses the\n    unparseable-input branch, which pass 1 has already adjudicated) — the\n    rules themselves are identical, which is the point: a skeleton is\n    judged by the same execution-position discipline as real text, so a\n    READ stays a read.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not command:\n        return None\n\n    # NO raw-substring pre-filter (codex S292 P1-A). The first implementation\n    # short-circuited on `basename in command`, which the SHELL defeats for\n    # free: `night\"-\"mode.py`, `night''-mode.py`, `night\\-mode.py` and\n    # `NIGHT-MODE.PY` all name the real file while the raw string does not\n    # contain the literal basename — every one returned ALLOW. Only the\n    # TOKENIZED, normalised form can answer \"does this run the toggle\", so\n    # the matcher now always tokenizes.\n    #\n    # Cost, measured on this repo (CPython 3.9.6, darwin): one `shlex` pass is\n    # 19-39us on a `decide_command` that already spends 76-188us — and the\n    # governing budget is the PLAN-089 §4 AC8 hook gate of p95 < 50ms per\n    # command (test_check_bash_safety_canonical_matrix.py). The added pass is\n    # ~0.06% of that budget, against a hook whose wall time is dominated by\n    # process spawn. `_e3` above already tokenizes unconditionally with the\n    # identical shlex configuration; sharing one memoized tokenizer between\n    # the two would make the marginal cost ~zero, and was NOT done here on\n    # purpose — it would put `_e3`'s whole matcher inside this patch's blast\n    # radius for 25us that the budget does not need.\n    try:\n        _lx = shlex.shlex(command, posix=True, punctuation_chars=\"();<>|&`\")\n        _lx.whitespace_split = True\n        tokens = list(_lx)\n    except ValueError:\n        # INPUT-parse failure → fail-CLOSED, but only for a command that\n        # plausibly names the toggle (PLAN-152 debate C4). The gate is the\n        # QUOTE/ESCAPE-STRIPPED, case-folded raw string: a broken command\n        # cannot be walked, so this is the widest sound test available, and\n        # it is the one shape where the raw scan is not defeatable by\n        # concatenation. Commands that do NOT name the toggle return None so\n        # they keep `_e3`'s own fail-closed parse message instead of a\n        # confusing night-mode reason.\n        #\n        # NOT on the expansion pass: pass 1 saw the operator's real text and\n        # already decided whether it parses. A skeleton that fails to parse\n        # where the original did is an artefact of `*` substitution, not an\n        # unparseable command, and denying on it would report a shape the\n        # operator never wrote.\n        if expansion_pass:\n            return None\n        flat = command.lower()\n        for _ch in (chr(39), chr(34), \"\\\\\"):\n            flat = flat.replace(_ch, \"\")\n        if any(b in flat for b in _E4_TOGGLE_BASENAMES_FOLDED):\n            return _e4_deny_reason(\"unparseable command naming the toggle\")\n        return None\n\n    n = len(tokens)\n    i = 0\n    at_cmd_word = True      # index 0, and every token after a TERMINATOR\n    chain_runner = None     # the prefix runner (exec/env/sudo/timeout/...) whose\n                            # flags we are currently parsing. Flags and numeric\n                            # operands before the real command word\n                            # (`timeout 5 python3 …`, `nice -n 10 python3 …`)\n                            # are part of the PREFIX, not of a new command.\n    ambiguous = False       # the previous token was a flag the runner's table\n                            # does not carry, so the NEXT token might be its\n                            # value rather than the command word. Fail-closed:\n                            # examine it as a command word AND keep scanning\n                            # one position further (codex S292 P1-B).\n    assigned_toggle = False  # a shell variable in THIS command holds the toggle\n                             # path (`S=.claude/scripts/night-mode.py; …`). shlex\n                             # does not expand `$S`, so the structural rules below\n                             # cannot see through it. Used ONLY in conjunction\n                             # with an execution position — `S=…; cat $S` stays\n                             # ALLOWED, because reading is not invoking.\n    while i < n:\n        tok = tokens[i]\n        if tok in _E3_TERMINATORS:\n            at_cmd_word = True\n            chain_runner = None\n            ambiguous = False\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        if tok in _E3_REDIRECTS:\n            i += 2          # skip the redirect operator + its target\n            continue\n        if not at_cmd_word:\n            # INTERPRETER HOP — the STRUCTURAL rail (codex S292 r5 P1).\n            #\n            # `xcrun python3 <toggle> on` was ALLOWED because `xcrun` is not\n            # in `_E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS`, so the walk stopped at it and never\n            # looked at `python3` again. Measured on this machine: it RUNS,\n            # and the script's own NF-08b self-path guard PASSES for it (the\n            # file executed is the canonical one), so this matcher is the\n            # ONLY rail for the shape. `arch`, `caffeinate` and `script`\n            # behaved identically, and the list had failed the same way twice\n            # before (r2 `env`/`sudo`, r4 braces).\n            #\n            # So stop asking WHO launches and ask WHAT is launched: after an\n            # unrecognised command word, keep testing every remaining token\n            # of the segment for INTERPRETER family. The launcher's name\n            # stops mattering — `<anything> python3 <toggle>` is denied.\n            #\n            # ONLY the family rule fires here, never the direct-exec, `$S` or\n            # substitution rules. That is the line between an invocation and a\n            # read: `cat <toggle>` and `xcrun <toggle>` are the SAME shape to\n            # a string matcher (the shell runs the first word in both; only\n            # the launcher's semantics differ), so applying the direct-exec\n            # rule at hopped positions would deny every read of the file.\n            # Direct exec through a launcher is covered — for the launchers\n            # that could be measured — by `_E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS` instead.\n            #\n            # FALSE POSITIVE, accepted and bounded: an interpreter NAME used\n            # as data in the same command as the toggle now denies, e.g.\n            # `grep -n python3 <toggle>`. It cannot be told apart from\n            # `xcrun python3 <toggle>` without knowing each command's\n            # semantics. It costs one rewrite (`cat <toggle> | grep python3`),\n            # it only ever fires on a command that already names the toggle,\n            # and the deny message says what to do — while the other error\n            # direction is a silent bypass of a rail three signed comments\n            # claim is closed (CLAUDE.md §4: fail-CLOSED on matcher input).\n            hop_family = _e4_runner_family(_e3_cmd_name(tok).lower())\n            if hop_family is not None:\n                hit = _e4_interpreter_deny(\n                    tokens, i, hop_family, assigned_toggle, expansion_pass)\n                if hit is not None:\n                    return hit\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        if _ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE.match(tok):\n            if _e4_is_toggle_path(tok.split(\"=\", 1)[1]):\n                assigned_toggle = True\n            i += 1          # leading NAME=VALUE — the command word is still ahead\n            continue\n        if chain_runner is not None and tok.startswith(\"-\"):\n            # Resolve the runner chain instead of stopping at the first\n            # non-flag token: `env -u FOO python3 <toggle>` used to read FOO\n            # as the command word and never look at python3 again.\n            _vals, _bools = _E4_PREFIX_RUNNER_FLAGS.get(\n                chain_runner, (frozenset(), frozenset()))\n            kind = _e4_classify_prefix_flag(tok, _vals, _bools)\n            if kind == \"value\" and i + 1 < n:\n                i += 2      # the next token is this flag's VALUE, not a command\n                continue\n            ambiguous = kind == \"unknown\"\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        if chain_runner is not None and _E4_DURATION_RE.match(tok):\n            i += 1          # `timeout 5 …` / `nice 10 …` — a prefix operand\n            continue\n        # CASE-FOLDED, for the same reason the PATH is (see\n        # `_e4_runner_family`): PATH lookup is case-insensitive on APFS, so\n        # `PYTHON3`/`SUDO`/`ENV` name the real binaries.\n        name = _e3_cmd_name(tok).lower()\n        if name in _E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS:\n            chain_runner = name\n            ambiguous = False\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        if _e4_is_toggle_path(tok):\n            return _e4_deny_reason(tok, expansion_pass)  # direct exec: `./…/night-mode.py on`\n        if assigned_toggle and \"$\" in tok:\n            return _e4_deny_reason(tok, expansion_pass)  # `S=…; $S on`\n        if tok in _E4_SUBST_OPENERS and _e4_substitution_body_names_toggle(tokens, i):\n            # `$(echo …/night-mode.py) on` — substitution IN COMMAND POSITION.\n            return _e4_deny_reason(tok, expansion_pass)\n        if name in _E4_LINK_RUNNERS:\n            # `ln [-s] <toggle> <alias>` — creating a second entry point.\n            # BOTH operand positions matter: source (the bypass) and target\n            # (replacing the toggle with a link, which `_e3` also denies as a\n            # write). Flags cannot match `_e4_is_toggle_path`, so the segment\n            # positionals are scanned as-is; no flag table is needed.\n            for operand in _e3_segment_positionals(tokens, i + 1):\n                if _e4_is_toggle_path(operand):\n                    return _e4_alias_deny_reason(operand, expansion_pass)\n                if assigned_toggle and \"$\" in operand:\n                    return _e4_alias_deny_reason(operand, expansion_pass)  # `S=…; ln -s $S X`\n            at_cmd_word = False\n            chain_runner = None\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        family = _e4_runner_family(name)\n        if family is not None:\n            hit = _e4_interpreter_deny(\n                tokens, i, family, assigned_toggle, expansion_pass)\n            if hit is not None:\n                return hit\n            at_cmd_word = False\n            chain_runner = None\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        if ambiguous:\n            # This token followed a flag the table does not know, so it may\n            # be that flag's VALUE and the command word may still