{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch74bjPodAoiXGf5REV1Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017MgaMLDDSP1WsKvDheu2Tg","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"report":"PAYLOAD 6/34. Files: .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/{posture_tables,rewrite_citation}.py; .claude/hooks/_lib/_budget_summary_parts/{audit_events,benchmark_native,cost_rollup,native_formatting,native_reconciliation,presentation_cli}.py; .claude/hooks/_lib/_ceo_boot_parts/{__init__,cache_recommendations,candidates_lessons}.py. I read them together with their entry points (.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py, .claude/scripts/budget-summary.py, .claude/scripts/ceo-boot.py). I compared the moved text line by line against the pre-split base text: the removed lines of .claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/diff-rc1-21.patch (check_bash_safety), diff-rc1-24.patch (budget-summary) and diff-rc1-26.patch (ceo-boot). The GA candidate equals the rc code.\n\nCHECKED, NO FINDING\n- rewrite_citation.py matches the base byte for byte in logic, from _FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE_CLASS to _extract_transcript_path. This covers the force-push rewrite (opt-in, default off), the escape hatch with two keys, the credential scan that fails closed, and the citation gate. The gate is default off, rejects plan-file citations, and fails closed on any read error. The cited text is redacted before it reaches the audit log.\n- Name resolution, Bash guard: every global the two parts use is defined in an earlier part. _trusted_env, _git_bypass, _credentials, _audit_emit, Rewrite, _ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE, hashlib, json, shlex, re and Path come from normalization.py. The _E3_* tables and _e3_* helpers come from fact_canonical.py. _e3_basename (fact_canonical.py:311) is defined before its module-level use at posture_tables.py:353. check_bash_safety.py:39 runs exec with globals == locals, so the generator expressions at module level resolve correctly. The delayed import `from check_canonical_edit import _CANONICAL_GUARDS, _fnmatch_segments` still resolves: guards.py's __all__ is bound at check_canonical_edit.py:118-122, and paths.__all__ through canonical_edit.bind (check_canonical_edit.py:619-620). If that import fails, _e3 returns None, which is fail-open on an infrastructure fault, unchanged. Input it cannot parse still fails closed (posture_tables.py:28-44, _scan_blob).\n- budget-summary: the rebinding loop (budget-summary.py:380-397) runs every part function in the entry point's globals. So __file__ resolves to .claude/scripts, and ceo-cost-transcripts.py and optimizer/model_normalize.py, which both exist, are found as before. The parts themselves have no top-level imports. With `from __future__ import annotations`, annotations are never evaluated. Default values use only constants local to each part (native_reconciliation.py:3-6, the same values). _lib is a regular package, so the subpackage without __init__.py imports as a namespace package on Python 3.9. Key logic lines (dated pricing, the codex model default, the cache tiers, the drift probe of the walk sweep, one shared now for both sources) match the base.\n- ceo-boot: install() (_ceo_boot_parts/__init__.py:21-26) runs each part in the entry point's dictionary. Every helper that cache_recommendations.py and candidates_lessons.py call is defined in state_plan_audit.py, scheduled_cache.py or telemetry_candidates.py. Path(__file__) resolves to .claude/scripts, where morning_ledger.py and lessons.py exist. cached_load/cached_store, both recommendation pipelines, _emit_task_candidate_markers (lock/persist semantics) and _validate_boot_lesson (fail-closed when the validator is unavailable) match the base. No hook runs ceo-boot.py or budget-summary.py, so neither can block a session.\n- Delivery: install.sh:1520-1549 copies every top-level _lib entry recursively.\n- Condition 5: check_arbitration_kernel.py:314 carries .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/**/*. Condition 7: the three entry points keep their paths, and the logic lives in _lib modules. Condition 6 is not touched by these files.\n- Standard library only. No 3.10+ syntax in the 11 files.\n\nP0: none. No declared condition is false against this payload.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): no undeclared P1.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS (new)\n1. P2 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_tables.py:267-268 (with fact_canonical.py:248, 288-308). The find branch of _e3 is narrowed, and this is not declared.\n   - Change: the base scanned `tokens[i + 1:]`, every later token (diff-rc1-21.patch:2458; staged copy .claude/plans/PLAN-182/staged-w1/tree/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py:2431). The candidate scans `_e3_segment_positionals(tokens, i + 1)`, which stops at the first terminator token.\n   - Why it leaks: with punctuation_chars, shlex returns the escaped `\\;` that closes a find -exec clause as the bare token \";\". That token is in _E3_TERMINATORS, so the scan stops at the end of the first -exec clause.\n   - Failure scenario: `find . -name nomatch -exec true \\; -o -path ./.claude/settings.json -delete`. The base blocks it (\"'find' invocation references canonical path\"). The candidate allows it: nothing after the \";\" matches an _e3 branch, _e4 sees no toggle, and bash_indirect_guard does not treat -delete or -fprint as writes. find then deletes the hook-registration file.\n   - Why P2: the narrowing is intended. It is the PLAN-195 round-1 consensus item 14, pinned by test_check_bash_safety_canonical_matrix.py:80-89 as a false-positive fix. It also adds no new capability, because the base already allowed `find .claude -maxdepth 1 -name settings.json -delete` and shell-glob spellings.\n   - Honesty: CHANGELOG [1.4.4] line 112 says the moved entry points keep \"their observable contracts\", and the section on the indirect guard (lines 91-94) presents the Bash guard change as adding blocks only. Neither mentions that the E3 matcher now allows commands the base blocked.\n   - Minimal fix: in the fin